.TH SCREENPORT 1 "2026-10-02" "ScreenPort 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
screenport \- graphical SSH client for named GNU screen sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B screenport
[\fB\-\-open\fR \fISERVER\fR [\fB\-\-screen\fR \fINAME\fR]...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ScreenPort
connects to SSH servers (password, private key or SSH agent) and opens
several terminals at once, each one attached to a GNU screen session with a
custom name. A missing session is created on the server; an existing one is
simply reattached.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-open " " \fISERVER\fR
Open the given saved server (name, identifier or user@host).
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-screen " " \fINAME\fR
With \fB\-\-open\fR, attach to (or create) this screen session directly.
May be repeated.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/screenport/servers.json
Saved servers (no secrets).
.TP
.I ~/.config/screenport/settings.json
Preferences and the list of tabs to restore.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SCREENPORT_DEBUG
Print the ssh command lines that are executed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR screen (1)
